  <article>
    <nav class="breadcrumb"><a href="/learn">Learn</a> / How to prove you can do the job</nav>
    <span class="mono-label">GUIDE</span>
    <h1>How do you prove you can do the job before you have the job?</h1>

    <p class="answer-capsule">You prove it with work, not words. The most reliable signal you can give an employer is a real, scored piece of the work the role involves, something they can open and judge before they hire you. It turns you from a list of claims on a résumé into evidence a recruiter can act on quickly.</p>

    <div class="tldr">
      <ul>
        <li>A résumé is a set of claims an employer has to trust. A work sample is evidence they can verify. Evidence wins.</li>
        <li>The strongest move is to do the actual work of the role and get it evaluated against a professional bar.</li>
        <li>Real work experience is the clearest protective factor in the data: an internship <a href="https://www.strada.org/reports/talent-disrupted" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cuts underemployment risk by 48.5%</a> (Strada and Burning Glass), and its value comes from the proof it leaves behind.</li>
        <li>You do not need an employer's permission to build that proof. You can produce scored work directly.</li>
        <li>This matters because <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/college-labor-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">41.5% of recent graduates are underemployed</a>, and proof is how you separate from the stack.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>Why does proof beat a résumé?</h2>
      <p>Picture a recruiter with two hundred applications and an afternoon. Every résumé is a list of claims: I am detail-oriented, I led a project, I am a strong communicator. The recruiter cannot check any of it, so they fall back on weak proxies like school name and keyword matches, and most applications get a few seconds before they move on. Now picture one applicant who attached a short, scored piece of real work in the exact role. The recruiter can open it and form a real judgment in about the same time it takes to read a bullet point. That applicant has changed the game from being believed to being evaluated, and evaluation is where a good candidate wins.</p>
      <p>This is the deeper reason the job market feels stuck for new graduates. The education system was built to filter people and hand them a degree, not to produce evidence that they can do specific work. So graduates arrive in the market with a credential that proves attendance and nothing that proves capability. Proof of work fills exactly that gap.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What does real proof actually look like?</h2>
      <p>Proof is an artifact, the kind a working professional produces, in the role you are targeting. If you want to do marketing, it is a positioning brief or a campaign plan with reasoning. If you want product, it is a spec that defines a problem, proposes a solution, names the trade-offs, and sets success metrics. If you want to work with data, it is a short analysis that ends in a clear recommendation. The format matters less than two properties: it has to be real, the kind of thing the job involves, and it has to be evaluated, so that its quality is established rather than asserted.</p>
      <p>That second property is what most people miss. A portfolio of unscored work tells a recruiter what you made, but not whether it is any good by a professional standard. A score from someone qualified to give it, plus the feedback behind that score, is what turns a work sample into trustworthy proof. It answers the question the recruiter is actually asking, which is not did you do something, but is your work good enough.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How do you build proof when you have no experience?</h2>
      <p>Work in a tight loop. Pick a single direction, do one real task in it, get that specific piece of work evaluated, fix it based on the feedback, then do the next one. Keep each loop short. While building zero, we ran a Middle East university pilot in Spring 2026, and the clearest finding was about exactly this loop. Students did not want heavy upfront instruction. They wanted to do something real within the first minute or two, and what brought them back was feedback on the work they had just submitted, not generic advice about the field. Short loops with real feedback build capability and proof at the same time, faster than studying ever could.</p>
      <p>You do not need a company to grant you this. An internship is the traditional way to get supervised real work, and the data shows how much it helps, but internships are scarce and the thing that makes them valuable is the evidence they leave behind. You can produce that same evidence directly by doing scored work on your own, which is the entire idea behind zero. You take on company-shaped tasks, your work is scored against a professional bar, and the scored result becomes a receipt a recruiter can evaluate.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How do you get your proof in front of recruiters?</h2>
      <p>Building proof is half the job. The other half is distribution, because a scored work sample sitting in a folder helps no one. The traditional answer is to paste links into applications and hope someone clicks, which mostly they do not. A better answer is to build your proof somewhere recruiters already look for evaluated talent. On zero, the scoring and the recruiter access are the same system: recruiters pay to find candidates who have already proven they can do the work, which is why it stays free for students. The point is to put your evidence where decisions get made, not where it gets ignored.</p>
    </section>

